# WHAT IS THIS FILE?
# A .dockerignore file tells Docker which files and folders to SKIP when it
# builds a container image from this project. When you run `docker build`,
# Docker's first step is to copy the whole project directory into the build
# context (a staging area it sends to the Docker engine) — anything listed
# here is excluded from that copy. This keeps the image smaller, the build
# faster, and prevents secrets or throwaway files (like logs or your local
# .env file) from accidentally ending up baked into a shipped image.
# Each non-comment line below is a "pattern" describing files/folders to
# ignore. A leading `/` anchors the pattern to the project root (instead of
# matching that name anywhere in the tree). A leading `!` means "actually,
# don't ignore this" — it re-includes something that a broader pattern above
# it would otherwise have excluded.

# `#` starts a comment in a .dockerignore file — everything after it on the
# line is ignored by Docker and is purely for humans reading the file.
# This comment points to Docker's own documentation for the full pattern
# syntax (wildcards, negation, etc.) in case you need something this file
# doesn't already demonstrate.
# See https://docs.docker.com/engine/reference/builder/#dockerignore-file for more about ignoring files.

# Blank line — used purely to visually separate this section from the next.
# Docker (and most config file formats) ignore blank lines entirely.

# Ignore git directory.
# `/.git/` excludes the entire hidden .git folder — that's git's internal
# database (every commit, branch, and history), which the running app never
# needs and which can be quite large. The trailing `/` means "this must be
# a directory" (as opposed to a file with the same name).
/.git/
# `/.gitignore` excludes the file you're looking at a sibling of — the git
# ignore-rules file itself. It's metadata for git, not something the app
# needs inside its container image.
/.gitignore

# Blank separator line.

# Ignore bundler config.
# `/.bundle` excludes the local Bundler configuration directory (if one
# exists). Bundler is Ruby's gem/dependency manager; `.bundle/config` can
# hold machine-specific settings (like custom gem install paths) that
# should NOT be copied into a container, which gets its own fresh Bundler
# setup during the Docker build.
/.bundle

# Blank separator line.

# Ignore all environment files.
# `/.env*` excludes any file starting with ".env" (the `*` is a wildcard
# matching zero or more of any character) — e.g. `.env`, `.env.local`,
# `.env.production`. These files typically hold secrets (API keys, database
# passwords) for local development and must never be baked into a shared
# Docker image, which could be pushed to a registry other people can pull.
/.env*

# Blank separator line.

# Ignore all default key files.
# `/config/master.key` excludes Rails' master encryption key — the secret
# used to decrypt config/credentials.yml.enc. If this leaked into a public
# image, anyone could decrypt all of the app's stored secrets.
/config/master.key
# `/config/credentials/*.key` excludes any per-environment key files inside
# config/credentials/ (Rails supports one master key per environment, e.g.
# production.key) — same reasoning: these are secrets, not app code.
/config/credentials/*.key

# Blank separator line.

# Ignore all logfiles and tempfiles.
# `/log/*` excludes everything currently inside the log/ directory (but not
# the log/ directory itself) — log files are generated output from running
# the app, not something needed to build or ship it.
/log/*
# `/tmp/*` excludes everything inside the tmp/ directory for the same
# reason — tmp/ holds caches, pid files, and other transient runtime data.
/tmp/*
# `!/log/.keep` re-includes one specific file: log/.keep. `.keep` is a
# common convention — an otherwise-empty placeholder file whose only job is
# to make git (and here, the Docker build) preserve an empty directory,
# since both git and Docker's COPY normally ignore empty folders entirely.
!/log/.keep
# `!/tmp/.keep` does the same thing for the tmp/ directory — keeps the
# empty folder present in the image even though its contents are ignored.
!/tmp/.keep

# Blank separator line.

# Ignore pidfiles, but keep the directory.
# `/tmp/pids/*` excludes the contents of tmp/pids/ — pid files (which store
# the process ID of a running server) are runtime state, regenerated fresh
# every time the container starts, so shipping stale ones would be useless
# or even harmful (a stale pid could be mistaken for a live process).
/tmp/pids/*
# `!/tmp/pids/.keep` re-includes the placeholder file so the tmp/pids/
# directory itself still exists in the built image, ready for the running
# app to write real pid files into later.
!/tmp/pids/.keep

# Blank separator line.

# Ignore storage (uploaded files in development and any SQLite databases).
# `/storage/*` excludes everything in storage/ — this is where Rails'
# Active Storage keeps locally-uploaded files, and where SQLite database
# files often live. None of that should be baked into the image; a fresh
# container should start with an empty storage/ and get its own database.
/storage/*
# `!/storage/.keep` keeps the empty storage/ directory present so the app
# has somewhere to write once it's actually running.
!/storage/.keep
# `/tmp/storage/*` excludes contents of a second, tmp-scoped storage
# folder (used for some test/dev-only file storage scenarios).
/tmp/storage/*
# `!/tmp/storage/.keep` keeps that folder's placeholder file so the empty
# directory still exists in the image.
!/tmp/storage/.keep

# Blank separator line.

# Ignore assets.
# `/node_modules/` excludes the node_modules folder (JavaScript package
# dependencies installed by npm/yarn, if this project uses any) — these are
# reinstalled fresh during the Docker build rather than copied from your
# local machine, where they might not even match the target OS/architecture.
/node_modules/
# `/app/assets/builds/*` excludes previously-compiled/bundled frontend
# assets (CSS/JS build output) sitting in your local working copy — the
# Docker build regenerates these itself so stale local builds don't leak in.
/app/assets/builds/*
# `!/app/assets/builds/.keep` keeps the empty builds/ directory present so
# the asset build step has somewhere to write its output during the build.
!/app/assets/builds/.keep
# `/public/assets` excludes any already-precompiled production assets
# (CSS/JS/images with cache-busting fingerprints in their filenames) that
# might exist locally — again, these get freshly regenerated in the image.
/public/assets

# Blank separator line.

# Ignore CI service files.
# `/.github` excludes the whole .github/ folder (GitHub Actions workflows,
# Dependabot config, issue templates, etc.) — none of this affects how the
# app runs, so there's no reason to bloat the image with it.
/.github

# Blank separator line.

# Ignore Kamal files.
# `/config/deploy*.yml` excludes Kamal deployment config files (e.g.
# config/deploy.yml, config/deploy.production.yml) — Kamal is a deploy tool
# that reads these from OUTSIDE the container (on your own machine or CI),
# so the container itself never needs a copy of its own deploy instructions.
/config/deploy*.yml
# `/.kamal` excludes Kamal's local working/cache directory for the same
# reason — it's tooling state for the deploy process, not app code.
/.kamal

# Blank separator line.

# Ignore development files
# `/.devcontainer` excludes the VS Code "Dev Containers" configuration
# folder — that's a convenience setup for local development environments,
# irrelevant to the production image this Dockerfile actually builds.
/.devcontainer

# Blank separator line.

# Ignore Docker-related files
# `/.dockerignore` excludes this very file from the build context — Docker
# doesn't need a copy of its own ignore-rules file inside the image.
/.dockerignore
# `/Dockerfile*` excludes any file starting with "Dockerfile" (the build
# instructions themselves, and variants like Dockerfile.dev) — the running
# container has no use for the recipe that built it.
/Dockerfile*
